3.3 Data Cleaning and Quality Control 

Rigorous cleaning protocols were implemented to handle missing values and administrative shifts, ensuring the integrity of the longitudinal panel.  

Imputation: Missing data points in non-critical years were handled via linear interpolation. However, counties with more than three consecutive years of suppressed data were excluded from the sample to avoid interpolation bias.  

Boundary Stability: The dataset was audited to ensure no county-level FIPS code changes or boundary shifts occurred during the 25-year study period. This step was essential to prevent geographic discontinuities that would otherwise bias the RDD framework.  

Outlier Detection: Specifically, counties showing sudden, idiosyncratic spikes in employment (unrelated to policy shifts, such as the opening of a single massive logistics hub) were flagged for sensitivity analysis.  

By addressing these data quality issues, the resulting dataset provided a stable foundation for the causal inference models. 

4. METHODOLOGY 

To provide a definitive answer to the "carbon leakage" question, this study employed a hybrid econometric strategy that leveraged both spatial and temporal variation. By integrating Geographic Regression Discontinuity (RDD) with a Difference-in-Differences (DiD) framework, the impact of the RGGI carbon tax was isolated from broader macroeconomic trends. 

